rn to avoid accidentally stripping partial matches.\n\nThe issue is that `replaceAll(\"#test\", \"\")` would incorrectly remove the `#test` portion from `#testa`, leaving just `a`. I need to use word boundary assertions or a negative lookahead to ensure I'm only matching complete tags, not partial matches within longer words.\n\nUsing `Pattern.quote()` to safely escape the tag text and adding `\\\\b` for word boundaries should fix this:\n\n```java\ntitle = title.replaceAll(Pattern.quote(tagToRemove.getText()) + \"\\\\b\", \"\");\ncontent = content.replaceAll(Pattern.quote(tagToRemove.getText()) + \"\\\\b\", \"\");\n```\n\nThis ensures that `#test` only matches as a complete word and won't accidentally strip it from `#testa` or other similar tags. I need to verify that `Pattern` is already imported in the file—it's there at line 31. Now I'm tracing through the `addTagToNote` method to understand how tags are being processed when a checkbox is unchecked. The method builds a list of tags to remove by checking which ones exist in the note and aren't in the selected tags list, then passes that to `removeTag` to handle the actual text replacement. So the flow is: identify unchecked tags that are in the note, collect them, then remove all occurrences of those tag texts from the title and content.\n\nThe real problem is that `removeTag` uses a naive string replacement that matches partial text—so removing \"#test\" would also strip it from \"#testa\" or \"#testb\". I need to make the removal more precise by only matching exact tag occurrences with word boundaries.\n\nNow I'm checking the test cases to understand the scope. There's a test that verifies tags are removed correctly, but it doesn't catch the partial match issue. Adding a new test case with adjacent similar tags would expose the bug, then I can apply the fix and verify both tests pass.\n\nWriting test cases... I'm realizing the test suite only has 4 tests and doesn't actually validate the specific bug scenario wh